<SiteLayout title="Google Ads vs Meta Ads for Lead Generation" description="Understand the different jobs Google Search and Meta Ads do in a lead generation strategy, and how to choose where to start." path="/insights/google-ads-vs-meta-ads-for-lead-generation/">
<article><header class="lf-article-hero"><div class="container"><p class="section-kicker"><a href="/insights/">INSIGHTS</a> / LEAD GENERATION</p><h1>Google Ads vs Meta Ads for Lead Generation</h1><p class="lf-lede">There is no universally better platform. The useful question is whether your prospective customer is already searching for a solution or needs to discover one.</p></div></header><div class="container lf-article-body"><section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Start with customer intent</h2><p>Google Search can reach people who are actively looking for a service. The quality of that demand depends on the terms, geography, landing page and competitiveness of the market.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Understand discovery on Meta</h2><p>Facebook and Instagram can place a relevant offer in front of people who are not currently searching. Creative clarity and the strength of the offer matter greatly here.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Compare the whole funnel</h2><p>Cost per click and cost per lead alone can mislead. Review qualified inquiries, contact rate, appointment rate and available sales outcomes.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Choose a test you can evaluate</h2><p>Start with one clear offer, usable tracking and sufficient time to learn. Avoid splitting a small budget across too many channels, audiences and creatives.</p></section><div class="lf-article-summary"><h2>What to do next</h2><p>Use Google when existing search intent is meaningful; test Meta when your offer can create interest through compelling creative. For many businesses, both play different roles.</p></div><a class="lf-back-link" href="/insights/">← Back to all insights</a></div></article>
</SiteLayout>